THE GOSPEL OF MARK AS A ROMAN FUNERARY COLLEGIUM CHARTER (c. 100 AD)

If the Gospel of Mark originated as the founding charter narrative of a Roman funerary collegium (collegium funeraticium / collegium tenuiorum) in Rome around 100 AD, every major textual anomaly that contradicts a Levantine origin is resolved, and the structure, vocabulary, and plot align directly with the social and legal mechanics of a Roman burial society.

======================================================================
1. STRUCTURAL & LITURGICAL CORROBORATIONS OF A FUNERARY COLLEGIUM
======================================================================

* Passion-to-Ministry Ratio (A Death-Centered Charter):
  In Mark, Jesus's public ministry is compressed into rapid, episodic bursts, while over one-third of the entire text (Chapters 11–16) slows down to hour-by-hour coverage of his arrest, execution, and burial. A Levantine wisdom movement or prophetic sect centers on teachings; a Roman funerary collegium exists specifically to commemorate the death and burial of its patron.

* Obsession With Proper Burial and Tomb Rituals:
  Mark frames Jesus's burial not as a postscript, but as a primary liturgical anchor:
  - Mark 14:3–9 (Pre-Death Anointing): An unnamed woman pours expensive nard over Jesus's head, and Jesus explicitly defines it as funerary preparation ("she has anointed my body beforehand for burial"), commanding that it be told wherever the message is proclaimed.
  - Mark 15:42–46 (Formal Legal Procurement of the Corpse): Joseph of Arimathea petitions Pilate directly for the body, wraps it in linen (sindon), places it in a rock-cut tomb, and seals it with a stone—mirroring the core legal function of a Roman collegium funeraticium, which existed to rescue corpses from mass pauper pits (puticuli) and secure dignified entombment.
  - Mark 16:1–2 (Post-Burial Tomb Visitation): Women bring aromatic spices to the tomb at dawn on the first day of the week—directly reflecting the Roman funerary custom of parentalia / rosalia and tomb-side memorial visits.

* The Memorial Banquet Script (Coena Funebris):
  Roman funerary collegia were legally permitted to meet monthly to pay dues and hold communal memorial meals honoring their deceased founder. Mark 14:22–25 (breaking bread as body, drinking wine as blood) establishes the exact ritual banquet formula, reinforced by repeated communal meal distribution stories (Mark 6:30–44; 8:1–10).

* Egalitarian Patronage & Replacement of Biological Family:
  In Rome, freedmen, slaves, and migrants lacked biological family networks to bury them; the collegium legally replaced the blood family. Mark systematically dismantles biological kinship in favor of the fictive association family: Jesus's biological family calls him insane (Mark 3:21), and Jesus explicitly repudiates his mother and brothers, declaring that the gathered circle around him are his true mother, brothers, and sisters (Mark 3:31–35), promising a hundredfold new network of houses, brothers, sisters, and mothers (Mark 10:29–30).

* Secrecy Discipline (Arcanum) Under Trajanic Collegia Bans:
  Roman authorities under Trajan (c. 98–117 AD) strictly policed unlicensed collegia as subversive political clubs (hetaeriai). Mark's pervasive "Messianic Secret" (repeatedly commanding healed individuals and insiders to tell no one, speaking in coded parables so outsiders "see but do not perceive" in Mark 4:11–12, and meeting in hidden upper rooms) reflects the operational security of an underground or marginalized Roman association protecting its membership from informants (delatores).

* Endurance Under State Execution & Torture:
  Unlike philosophical schools, members of illicit associations in Rome faced summary execution by crucifixion or damnatio ad bestias. Mark explicitly prepares its members for Roman judicial interrogation and execution: being beaten in councils, standing before governors and kings (Mark 13:9), betrayal by family members (Mark 13:12), and the literal demand to "take up your cross" (Mark 8:34).

